  "content": {
    "id": "p28-q7-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "7",
    "prompt": "A bag contains black and white balls. If two balls are randomly selected without replacement, then the probability that both are black is $\\frac{7}{20}$. On the other hand, if two balls are selected with replacement, then the probability that both are black is $\\frac{9}{25}$. How many black balls are in the bag?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "15 black balls.\n\nUse the replacement probability to find the proportion of black balls, then use the probability without replacement to determine the count.",
      "worked": "Let $b$ be the number of black balls and $N$ the total number of balls. With replacement,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\left(\\frac{b}{N}\\right)^2&=\\frac{9}{25}\\\\\\frac{b}{N}&=\\frac35.\\end{align*}$$\nWrite $b=3k$ and $N=5k$. Without replacement,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{3k}{5k}\\times\\frac{3k-1}{5k-1}&=\\frac7{20}\\\\12(3k-1)&=7(5k-1)\\\\36k-12&=35k-7\\\\k&=5\\\\b&=3\\times5\\\\b&=15.\\end{align*}$$\nThere are 15 black balls.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 62
  },
